[%# cloud-init network-config v2 for a guest, rendered by
    Provisioner::Recipe::ubuntu.  cloud-init hands v2 to netplan nearly as
    written.

    It has two interfaces.  The outbound bridge carries the addresses that
    reach this guest.  The libvirt NAT is how the hypervisor finds the guest
    before those addresses work.  A hypervisor whose block names nics gives the
    guest an adapter on each of its other bridges too, which comes up with no
    address.  See nics in Trog::HV::Libvirt.

    Each interface matches on its MAC address, not on its name.  The names below
    are what the guest calls each interface.  We choose the MAC address and the
    guest cannot change it, so the MAC address decides which card is which.

    No apostrophes in an Xslate comment.  The lexer reads one as the start of a
    string and swallows the template as far as the next quote. -%]
---
[% IF !bridge_mac -%]
[%# Nothing here defined this machine, so we do not know its MAC addresses or
    interface names.  A service assigned them when it created the guest.  A
    configuration cannot match on a MAC address that nobody gave us.  The
    platform already gave the guest its addresses, so tell cloud-init to leave
    the network alone. -%]
network:
  config: disabled
[% ELSE -%]
network:
  version: 2
  ethernets:
    [% bridge_devname | yaml %]:
      match:
        macaddress: [% bridge_mac | yaml %]
      set-name: [% bridge_devname | yaml %]
      nameservers:
        search:
          - [% domain | yaml %]
        addresses:
[% FOR resolver IN resolvers -%]
          - [% resolver | yaml %]
[% END -%]
[% IF addresses.size() -%]
      addresses:
[% FOR address IN addresses -%]
        - [% address | yaml %]
[% END -%]
[%# The bins of ip_pool that another guest routes to, such as the static
    addresses of VPN clients.  See Provisioner::IPPool.  Once for the interface,
    because netplan refuses the same route twice. -%]
      routes:
        - to: default
          via: [% gateway | yaml %]
[% FOR r IN bin_routes -%]
        - to: [% r.to | yaml %]
          via: [% r.gateway | yaml %]
[% END -%]
[% END -%]
[%# Up, with no address, and optional, so that the boot does not wait for an
    address that nothing gives it. -%]
[% FOR nic IN other_nics -%]
    [% nic.name | yaml %]:
      match:
        macaddress: [% nic.mac | yaml %]
      set-name: [% nic.name | yaml %]
      optional: true
[% END -%]
[%# With no ips, the bridge gets no addresses and no routes, so it comes up with
    no address.  The guest takes its lease and its default route on the NAT
    side.  See offers in Provisioner::IPPool.

    The NAT side sends its MAC as the DHCP client identifier.  The default is
    a DUID made from the machine-id, which every rebuild makes new, so dnsmasq
    took a rebuilt guest for a new client and leased it a new address.  With the
    MAC, which comes from the name of the domain, it gets back the lease it had. -%]
    [% dhcp_devname | yaml %]:
      match:
        macaddress: [% nat_mac | yaml %]
      set-name: [% dhcp_devname | yaml %]
      dhcp4: true
      dhcp-identifier: mac
[% END -%]
